<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<TcPlcObject Version="1.1.0.1">
  <POU Name="F_ConvertTicksToUnixTimestamp" Id="{33156588-fcdd-4f41-9fa6-98babd8a3abc}" SpecialFunc="None">
    <Declaration><![CDATA[(*
    F_ConvertTicksToUnixTimestamp - convert Windows FILETIME ticks to Unix time.

    PURPOSE
        Converts the timestamp of a Tc3 event, as delivered by the
        Tc3_EventLogger function blocks (fbEvent.nTimestamp), into the Unix
        timestamp the logging consumers of this library work with, so events
        raised in the PLC can be ordered and correlated with the rest of the
        control system.

    USAGE
        Called by FB_Listener while it copies a received Tc3 event into a
        pending log record:
            stEventInfo.ts := F_ConvertTicksToUnixTimestamp(fbEvent.nTimestamp);
        The function is pure - no internal state and no side effects - so it may
        be called from any task context. The argument must be a FILETIME tick
        count as described on nTimestamp below: a TwinCAT TIME value is not
        accepted, and no time-zone shift is applied (the result is UTC).

    OBJECTIVES
        - Keep the 1601-to-1970 epoch shift and the 100 ns tick resolution in
          one named conversion instead of repeating the magic numbers at every
          call site.
        - Return LREAL so the fractional second survives; rounding to an
          integer would lose the sub-second ordering of events logged within
          the same second.
        - State the tick base and units in the interface, so a caller cannot
          silently pass a TIME or an already-Unix value.

    HISTORY
        2019-11-12 / K Lauer
            Function added with the Tc3_EventLogger logging rewrite.
        2026-11-27 / Scirix Agent
            Added this header comment and the nTimestamp declaration comment;
            documentation only, no behaviour change (audit finding #89,
            convention conv_321640e095c0).

    NOTE
        The constant declarations below are the authority for the tick rate and
        the epoch offset used by the conversion.
*)
FUNCTION F_ConvertTicksToUnixTimestamp : LREAL
VAR_INPUT
    // Windows FILETIME ticks (100 ns units) counted from 1601-01-01 UTC,
    // i.e. the tick base of Tc3_EventLogger (fbEvent.nTimestamp).
    // Divide by 10 000 000 for seconds, then subtract 11 644 473 600 s
    // to move the epoch to 1970-01-01 UTC. Not a TwinCAT TIME value.
    nTimestamp : ULINT;
END_VAR
VAR CONSTANT
    // Timer ticks in Windows are 100ns (1e-7 sec)
    nTicksToSeconds : LREAL := 10_000_000;
    // Epoch offset 1601 to 1970
    nEpochOffset	: LREAL := 11_644_473_600;
END_VAR
]]></Declaration>
    <Implementation>
      <ST><![CDATA[F_ConvertTicksToUnixTimestamp := ULINT_TO_LREAL(nTimestamp) / nTicksToSeconds - nEpochOffset;]]></ST>
    </Implementation>
  </POU>
</TcPlcObject>